## Suko, generated from the Rosie teaching script (english-input.txt) under the world conventions.
##
## Instructor statements used (numbers as in english-input.txt):
##   1. The name of the puzzle is suko.
##   4. The name of the action is write-1-to-9.
##   5. You can write a number that is between one and nine onto an empty location.
##   6. If the value of a location is absent then the location is empty.
##   7. The solution has four steps.
##   9. The name of the goal is all-filled.
##  11. The goal is that all locations are filled.
##  12. If the value of a location is more than zero then the location is filled.
##  14. If the value of a location is the value of another location then you lose.
##  16. If the locations in a grouping are filled and the sum of the values of the grouping is not the value of a block that is above the grouping then you lose.
##  18. If a block is filled and their locations are under the block then they are in a grouping.
##  19. If the blue locations are filled and the sum of the values of them is not ten then you lose.
##  21. If the red locations are filled and the sum of the values of them is not nine then you lose.
##  23. If the green locations are filled and the sum of the values of them is not twenty-six then you lose.
## World: init-suko.world (a 3x3 grid of locations and four clue blocks, each above the four locations around it)
##
## Translation (tools/rosie-language.md gives the general reading; the assumption ledger is in README.md):
##   - grouping: the locations under one block (the under relation of the world file); target: that block's value.
##   - "the blue (red, green) locations are filled and the sum of the values of them is not ten (nine, twenty-six)": a group per color with a constant target.
##   - Operator write (argument1 = the location, argument2 = number-k for k from 1 to 9).

sp {suko*propose*initialize-suko
   (state <s> ^superstate nil ^world <w> -^name)
-->
   (<s> ^operator <o> + >)
   (<o> ^name initialize-suko ^task-operator false)
}

